# Interpretation and next steps

The analyses address different questions. Monthly and period reporting ratios compare report counts with distribution volume. RORs compare the composition of reports across products. Neither measure estimates causal risk, and agreement between them would not establish causality.

I have not yet adjusted the comparisons for species, country or time. Next, I will examine whether prioritized patterns persist within these strata and assess the effect of sparse cells and comparator choice. Where relevant, I will inspect batch-level counts and event composition. Batch-specific incidence cannot be estimated because batch-level dose denominators are unavailable.

This dataset simplifies reporting to one animal, one suspect product and one primary event per case. Its event terms and seriousness rules are training definitions. Complete clinical narratives are unavailable. The simulation deliberately introduced distribution growth, seasonality and selected product-event or batch-event patterns. Recovering those patterns demonstrates the workflow on this dataset, not its performance on real surveillance data.

# Reproducibility

I organized the work into four scripts covering preparation, monthly trends, period comparisons and disproportionality. This report reads their saved RDS results and PNG figures without rerunning the analyses. When an analysis changes, its downstream scripts and figures must be regenerated before I render the report again. Each analysis folder contains its own session information; these records describe the analysis environment rather than only the reporting session.

